k04 -- d log a_0 / d log Upsilon FOR THE CLOSED-FORM INVERSION  a_0 = (1-f_DM) g_obs / [ln(1/f_DM)]^2
========================================================================================================================

    f_DM :    0.10    0.20    0.30    0.40    0.50    0.60    0.70    0.80    0.90    0.95
    lever:   19.98    9.96    6.61    4.92    3.89    3.18    2.66    2.24    1.87    1.67     (analytic, 1 + 2/ln(1/f_bar))
    check:   20.03    9.97    6.61    4.92    3.89    3.18    2.66    2.24    1.87    1.67     (finite difference of the estimator)
  [PASS] k04-1 the analytic lever must reproduce a finite difference of the estimator itself to 1%   (max relative difference 2.41e-03)
  [PASS] k04-2 the lever must EXCEED 2 wherever the system is more than half baryonic -- i.e. the estimator amplifies a mass-to-light error rather than damping it, in exactly the regime the high-redshift rotation-curve surveys occupy   (f_DM = 0.5 -> lever 3.89; f_DM = 0.2 -> 9.96; f_DM = 0.1 -> 19.98)
  [PASS] k04-3 and it must FALL toward order unity in the deep-MOND, dark-dominated regime, where the estimator is safe   (f_DM = 0.90 -> lever 1.87; f_DM = 0.95 -> 1.67; asymptote as f_DM -> 1 is 1.00)

========================================================================================================================
WHAT IT COSTS THE HUNT'S OWN STRONGEST RESULT (item 16), ON ITS OWN TABLE
========================================================================================================================
  RC100, N = 100: median f_DM(<R_e) = 0.290  ->  median Upsilon lever = 6.85
     z = 0.5-1.2:  N =  32   median f_DM = 0.395   median lever = 4.99
     z = 1.2-1.8:  N =  27   median f_DM = 0.270   median lever = 7.36
     z = 1.8-2.6:  N =  41   median f_DM = 0.260   median lever = 7.64
  
  THE LEVER ITSELF RISES WITH REDSHIFT: d(lever)/dz = +1.91 per unit z, because RC100's
  galaxies get more baryon-dominated with redshift -- which is the very trend item 16 is reading.
  Consequence, and it is the sharp one: a CONSTANT stellar mass-to-light error does NOT cancel out of
  the slope, because it is amplified by a different factor at each redshift.  A Upsilon offset of
  delta dex propagates into d log a_0/dz as delta x +1.91, so:
     a constant Upsilon offset of 0.02 dex -> spurious d log a_0/dz = +0.038   (against item 16's quoted -0.112 +- 0.063)
     a constant Upsilon offset of 0.03 dex -> spurious d log a_0/dz = +0.057   (against item 16's quoted -0.112 +- 0.063)
     a constant Upsilon offset of 0.05 dex -> spurious d log a_0/dz = +0.096   (against item 16's quoted -0.112 +- 0.063)
     a constant Upsilon offset of 0.10 dex -> spurious d log a_0/dz = +0.191   (against item 16's quoted -0.112 +- 0.063)
  
  A 0.03 dex constant offset in Upsilon -- well inside stellar-population uncertainty -- reproduces
  the whole of item 16's statistical error bar, and a 0.06 dex offset reproduces its entire signal.
  REPORTED AGAINST THE HUNT'S OWN INTEREST: item 16's d log a_0/dz = -0.112 +- 0.063 carries an
  unquantified systematic of at least the size of its statistical error, and its '3.9 sigma against
  the LambdaCDM-native rise' must be requoted with that systematic attached.  The sign of the effect
  is not settled by this item: a younger (lower-Upsilon) high-z population raises f_DM and lowers the
  inferred a_0, which pushes the SAME way as the measurement, so the conclusion may survive -- but it
  is no longer a systematics-free number.
  [PASS] k04-4 the amplification is redshift-DEPENDENT on RC100's own table, so a constant mass-to-light error does not cancel out of the slope the item quotes.  This check fails the naive claim that a slope is systematics-free   (d(lever)/dz = +1.91 per unit z; a 0.03 dex constant Upsilon offset -> +0.057 spurious d log a_0/dz against a quoted -0.112 +- 0.063)

